#{[17592186166705 :question/worked-solution "![](images/f1/f187eff194f136838b67ab12b44f8f6882eafb59885a5c3f440d41492def4a42.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\sin^2 x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\sin^2 x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$"] [17592186167067 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186163480] [17592186167067 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186375708] [17592186167069 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186163492] [17592186167069 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186378389] [17592186167070 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186163498] [17592186167070 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186378395] [17592186167223 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = - 4 y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 9 x ( t )\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1, 2} = ± 6\\textrm{i}$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1, 2} = [± 2\\textrm{i}, 3]^{T}$, respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sin (6t) \\\\ 3\\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cos (6t) \\\\ 3\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when $c_{1} = 1$ and $c_{2} = 0$, we obtain the following curve:\n$3\\sin (6t)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167224 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 2 x ( t ) - 3 y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 3 x ( t ) + 2 y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1, 2} = 2 ± 3\\textrm{i}$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1, 2} = [± \\textrm{i}, 1]^{T}$, respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} = c_{1}e^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) \\\\ \\sin (3t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}e^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin (3t) \\\\ \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when $c_{1} = 1$ and $c_{2} = 0$, we obtain the following curve:\n$e^{2t}\\sin (3t)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167230 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=9x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =1\\pm 6\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186167230 :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=1>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$"] [17592186167242 :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{infinity}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty ,$ we require $a>0.$"] [17592186167243 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=5x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186167243 :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-2<0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable spiral (sink)}.$"] [17592186168010 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\pm4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 1]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]} = \nc_1 e^{t/2} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\cos   ( 4 t ) \\\\ \\sin   ( 4 t )\\end{array} \\right]} + \nc_2 e^{t/2} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\sin   ( 4 t ) \\\\ \\cos   ( 4 t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R},\n$$\nwe are interested in sketching the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above.\n\nIf we set $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$ in the general solution of the system, we obtain the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.22$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9da4b66977b98b983477d22abb6e00135ea0e1bfa9f785cd0a62d674a7b782.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(4t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(4t)| \\leq 1,$ but $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the obtained curve is unbounded. So, we have a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nAgain, since $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ the spiral is traversed toward infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62380812d6fbe4e28531a455814296a4969a0744fb28a94a5e2d7c588ec5cfc4.png)"]}
